          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does antibiotic stewardship guidance say about upper respiratory infections?",
          "answer": "Guidance on upper respiratory infections (URIs) is clear on the main point: most of these infections should not be treated with antibiotics. The main source is the guidance titled \"Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated.\"\n\n**The default position.** That guidance says the common cold, most sore throats, acute bronchitis, and uncomplicated sinusitis in its first days are viral in most cases. It states that antibiotics do not shorten these illnesses and do not prevent complications at a rate that justifies routine use. It also lists the harms: side effects, allergic reactions, *C. difficile* infection, and antibiotic resistance. It describes not prescribing as the active recommendation, and says that explaining the expected course and offering symptom relief are part of the care.\n\n**Where authorities disagree.** The same guidance reports two disagreements:\n- Some bodies recommend a delayed-prescription strategy as a tool to reduce immediate prescribing. Others are wary, because it still leads to a prescription in many cases and may reinforce the idea that these illnesses need antibiotics.\n- National guidance differs on sore-throat management. Some places more weight on clinical scoring and less on routine testing before treatment.\n\n**Evidence strength.** The retrieved passages do not grade the evidence behind these recommendations, so I cannot tell you how strong it is. The guidance does say that antibiotics do not meaningfully help in most of these cases, but it does not put a grade on that.\n\n**What I could not find.** The search did not return specific guidance on acute sinusitis or ear infection (otitis media), so I cannot say what stewardship guidance advises for those conditions. A clinician or a more specific search would be needed for them.\n\nThis is general information, not advice about any particular illness. If you are deciding whether to seek or take an antibiotic, a clinician who knows your history is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
